dles HEAD by not sending the body (Content-Length is still computed only if you set it or if body is small enough to buffer). Safer to phrase as \"verify HEAD behavior and set Content-Length explicitly\" rather than assert.)\n\nI should avoid over-asserting subtle Go internals. Keep it accurate and general.\n\nFormat: markdown, headers, tables, a prompt template in a code block. Keep it reasonably tight but this is a planning deliverable so moderate length is fine. Aim for scannable structure.\n\nLet me draft the answer.\n\nStructure:\n- Quick framing: what the assignment really grades → split into \"baseline\" and \"improvement delta\", so plan around capturing both.\n- Key decision: two implementation tracks (table).\n- Phase plan (0-5) with deliverables + time.\n- Prompting playbook: 10 tips, condensed, plus a copy-paste prompt template.\n- Baseline acceptance checklist (curl matrix).\n- Improvement backlog mapped to course topics (table).\n- Evidence/report structure.\n- Common failure modes.\n\nThen the question.\n\nLet me write the prompt template carefully.\n\n```\nYou are a Go engineer. Produce a single-file HTTP server for a networks course baseline.\n\nEnvironment:\n- Go 1.22+, module `httpserver`, stdlib only, no third-party packages\n- One file: main.go, plus README.md with run + curl examples\n- Must pass: go build ./..., go vet ./...\n\nHTTP surface (exact):\n- GET /            -> 200, text/plain, body \"ok\"\n- GET /health      -> 200, application/json, {\"status\":\"ok\"}\n- GET /echo        -> 200, echoes query param `msg`; missing -> 400\n- POST /echo       -> 201, echoes JSON body; invalid JSON -> 400, body > 1MB -> 413\n- anything else    -> 404 with JSON error envelope\n- unsupported method on known path -> 405 with Allow header\n\nRules:\n- Explicit http.Server with ReadHeaderTimeout, ReadTimeout, WriteTimeout, IdleTimeout, MaxHeaderBytes\n- Errors via a single helper that sets status + JSON body, never writes 200 first\n- No global state, no D