          "text": "## Framing: the assignment grades the *delta*, not the server\n\nThe rubric almost certainly rewards \"AI produced a baseline → I applied networking knowledge to improve it → I can prove the difference.\" So plan around **capturing and evidencing two artifacts**:\n\n1. a frozen, working baseline the AI generated, with the prompt log that produced it;\n2. a committed improvement set, each change tied to a specific concept from lecture (HTTP/1.1 semantics, TCP behavior, concurrency, timeouts).\n\nIf you only ship a nice server, you lose the process credit. Protect the baseline: commit it *unmodified* on day one under a `baseline` tag before touching anything.\n\n## One decision up front: which layer the AI builds on\n\n| Track | Baseline the AI writes | Networking value | Risk |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A. stdlib (`net/http`)** | Handlers + explicit `http.Server` config | Semantics, status codes, timeouts, keep-alive — but Go does the wire work for you | Project may look \"too easy\"; improvements must be chosen carefully |\n| **B. from scratch (`net.Conn`)** | `Accept` loop, hand-written request-line/header parser, body reads | Real HTTP/1.1 message framing, keep-alive loop, chunked encoding, slowloris | More code, more failure modes, harder to get compile-clean from one prompt |\n| **C. Hybrid (recommended)** | AI generates Track A baseline; your improvement phase ports the request loop to Track B | Best narrative: \"AI gave me a working server; course knowledge let me replace the black box with my own parser\" | Scope control needed |\n\nTrack C is the strongest story for a networks class and is usually what earns the \"improvements\" marks. Pick the ceiling deliberately — don't let the AI choose.\n\n## Phase plan\n\n**Phase 0 — Contract (before you prompt).** Write down, in your own words: routes, methods, status codes, headers, body rules, and non-goals (no TLS, no auth, no DB, no Docker, no third-party deps). This document *is* your prompt spec. If you can't write the contract, you can't grade the AI's output.\n\n**Phase 1 — Generate the baseline in slices.** Never one mega-prompt. Five turns, each compiling:\n1. server skeleton + explicit `http.Server` + graceful shutdown\n2. routing and the status-code contract\n3. error envelope helper (single place that sets status before body)\n4. bounded request bodies, header limits, timeouts\n5. request logging with method/path/status/duration\n\n**Phase 2 — Verify the baseline like a grader.** Build a curl/`Invoke-WebRequest` matrix that proves every status code and header, and run it before you change a line. Anything the AI claimed but didn't deliver goes straight into your \"what the AI got wrong\" section — that's graded material.\n\n**Phase 3 — Improvement backlog from lecture knowledge.** Choose 5–7, each with a one-line justification you could say out loud in a viva:\n\n| Improvement | Concept it demonstrates |\n|---|---|\n| Timeouts set deliberately (`ReadHeaderTimeout`, `IdleTimeout`, `MaxHeaderBytes`) | slowloris / resource exhaustion at TCP level |\n| Correct 405 + `Allow` header, 415, 413, 414, 501 | HTTP status semantics, not just 200/404 |\n| `Content-Length` vs chunked, `Date`, `Connection: close`, hop-by-hop headers | HTTP/1.1 message framing |\n| Host header required on 1.1, request-line/header size caps, malformed request → 400 | RFC-conformant parsing |\n| Graceful shutdown: close listener, drain connections, SIGINT | TCP connection lifecycle |\n| Concurrency limit (semaphore) + keep-alive reuse; measure with `hey`/`wrk` | concurrency, throughput vs latency |\n| Hand-rolled request parser on `net.Conn` (Track C payoff) | end-to-end HTTP/1.1 on TCP |\n| Wireshark capture of one request/response | protocol evidence for the report |\n\n**Phase 4 — Measure and report.** Table of baseline vs improved: status-code correctness, throughput, p50/p95 latency, goroutine count under load, keep-alive vs `Connection: close`. Include the prompt log, the diff summary, and a short \"what AI got wrong and how course knowledge fixed it.\"\n\n## Prompting playbook\n\n1. **Prompt a deliverable, not a vibe.** \"Basic HTTP server in Go\" gets you generic code. Give a route/status table.\n2. **Pin the stack** in the prompt: Go version, module name, stdlib only, file layout. Otherwise you get Gin, Dockerfiles, and a config loader.\n3. **State non-goals explicitly** — the single biggest lever against scope creep.\n4. **Demand acceptance evidence in the same turn**: a curl matrix showing each status code. This turns \"looks right\" into \"provably right.\"\n5. **Ask for an \"Assumptions & omissions\" section.** Have the AI list what it skipped and which HTTP/TCP behaviors a reviewer should scrutinize. That list is your improvement backlog, handed to you for free.\n6. **Slice, don't dump.** One layer per prompt, compile between turns.\n7. **Gate every turn**: `go build ./...` and `go vet ./...` clean, no unused code, no invented APIs.\n8. **Make it explain.** \"Walk me through the request lifecycle and quiz me on three things a grader would ask.\" Cheap, and it protects you in a viva.\n9. **Keep a prompt log** (prompt, model, output, what you rejected and why). Same prompt on a second model is a great comparison data point.\n10. **Never commit code you can't defend.** If you can't explain a line, either learn it or delete it.\n\nCopy-paste starter:\n\n```\nRole: Go engineer. Deliverable: baseline HTTP server for a networks course.\n\nEnvironment\n- Go 1.22+, module `httpserver`, stdlib only, no third-party packages\n- main.go + README.md. Must pass `go build ./...` and `go vet ./...`\n\nHTTP surface (exact)\n- GET  /        -> 200 text/plain \"ok\"\n- GET  /health  -> 200 application/json {\"status\":\"ok\"}\n- GET  /echo    -> 200, echoes ?msg=; missing -> 400\n- POST /echo    -> 201, echoes JSON body; bad JSON -> 400; body > 1MB -> 413\n- unknown path  -> 404 JSON error envelope\n- unsupported method on known path -> 405 with Allow header\n\nRules\n- Explicit http.Server with ReadHeaderTimeout, ReadTimeout, WriteTimeout,\n  IdleTimeout, MaxHeaderBytes; listen on :8080\n- One error helper that sets status + JSON body; never write 200 before WriteHeader\n- No DefaultServeMux, no global handler registration\n- Log method, path, status, duration per request\n- Non-goals: TLS, auth, DB, Docker, frameworks, config files\n\nDeliver\n1. main.go\n2. README.md: build/run + a curl matrix proving every status code\n3. \"Assumptions & omissions\": what you left out, and which HTTP/TCP\n   behaviors a reviewer should scrutinize\n```\n\n**Predictable AI failures to check for:** missing/ignored timeouts; `WriteHeader` after body writes; 405 without `Allow`; unbounded request bodies; no graceful shutdown; `DefaultServeMux` global state; logging without status/duration; silent extra middleware. Each one you catch is a paragraph in your report."
