ter on a parser *you wrote* is the strongest possible evidence for a networks course.\n\n**Phase 5 — Evidence.** Attack table, Wireshark capture of a smuggling attempt, benchmark deltas, prompt log.\n\n## The 5–7 security improvements\n\n| # | Hardening | Attack it defeats | Evidence to show |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `ReadHeaderTimeout`, `IdleTimeout`, `MaxHeaderBytes`, per-IP connection cap | slowloris, header flood | 500 half-open sockets; server stays responsive |\n| 2 | Strict framing validation: reject `Content-Length` + `Transfer-Encoding`, duplicate/conflicting CL, obfuscated TE, obs-fold, control chars in headers | request smuggling, header injection | Raw-socket payloads → 400 instead of desync |\n| 3 | Never interpolate user input into headers; validate before `Location`/redirect | response splitting | `?next=%0d%0aSet-Cookie:x=y` → rejected |\n| 4 | `http.MaxBytesReader` + bounded allocation | memory exhaustion DoS | 2 GB body → 413, RSS flat |\n| 5 | Path safety: `http.Dir`/`ServeContent` or `filepath.Clean` + `filepath.Rel` root check, reject symlink escape | directory traversal | `/../`, `%2e%2e%2f`, double-encoded variants → 404 |\n| 6 | Generic error envelopes, no stack traces, neutralized `Server` header, escaped logs | information disclosure, log injection | Diff error bodies; `\\n` in path doesn't forge log lines |\n| 7 | TLS `MinVersion: TLS1.2`, HSTS, `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, CSP, `Cache-Control` on sensitive routes; constant-time token compare with `crypto/subtle` | MITM, sniffing/XSS-in-class, brute force, timing leak | `openssl s_client` handshake; Wireshark shows no plaintext |\n\nPick 6 of these. **#2 and #4 are non-negotiable** — they're the ones a networking instructor will probe. #7 only if TLS is in scope for the course.\n\n## Prompt adjustment for a security-led baseline\n\nAdd one block to the prompt template:\n\n```\nSecurity posture\n- Implement only obvious hygiene (bounded body, generic errors). Do NOT add TLS,\n  auth